course: dictionary
lesson: 12098
title: Coercion: when a choice is not free
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you not just what happened but whether anyone had a real choice. Coercion is one of them. It means making someone do something by using force or threats, so they have no real choice. «The king used coercion to make the villagers pay, threatening to burn their homes.» The villagers could have said no, but the threat of fire took that choice away.
explain: Look at the clues in the sentence. Threatening to burn homes is a threat, and paying is what the king wanted. Together they show coercion. Always ask: did this person agree freely, or were they pushed? Swap in your meaning, «forced by threats», and check it still makes sense.
explain: Coercion can happen in everyday life too, including online. «Nobody should use coercion to make you share a photo.» This is a safety rule. If someone threatens you to get what they want, that is not fair, and you should tell a trusted adult straight away.
fact: Coercion means making someone do something by force or threats, so they have no real choice.
fact: Coercion is the opposite of free consent: a yes given under pressure is not a real yes.
fact: In law, coercion can mean pressure that is against the law, such as forcing someone to sign something.
fact: «Coercive control» is a pattern of threats and pressure used to control someone.
fact: If someone threatens you to get what they want, tell a trusted adult straight away.
exercise: «The king used coercion to make the villagers pay.» What does coercion mean? | Asking someone politely for help | Giving someone a gift to say thank you | *Forcing someone to do something with threats, so they have no real choice | Forgetting to pay
exercise: «Nobody should use coercion to make you share a photo online.» Why is this a safety rule? | Because photos are too heavy to send | Because online is only for adults | Because it is always polite to share | *Because a choice made under threats is not a free choice
exercise: «He signed the form only because of threats, not by choice.» Find and copy the word that shows the signing was not free. | *threats | choice | only | signed
exercise: Which phrase means pressure used to control someone with threats? | Gentle hugs | *Coercive control | Free time | Kind words
exercise: Zara says, «I lent my phone because my friend threatened to tell my secret.» Which word best describes this? | Generosity | Kindness | Curiosity | *Coercion
exam: Is this a free choice or coercion? «Ava agreed to lend her phone because her friend threatened to tell her secret.» | A joke | *Coercion | A gift | Free choice
